So what's the difference?

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.775
-I guess the straight edges on
the box, but you don't have

00:01:36.775 --> 00:01:38.275
straight edges on the sphere.

00:01:38.275 --> 00:01:39.775
-Well, I did when I
took it down to that.

00:01:39.775 --> 00:01:41.275
I do.

00:01:41.275 --> 00:01:42.775
Right?

00:01:42.775 --> 00:01:44.275
So there's all
straight edges again.

00:01:44.275 --> 00:01:46.535
This is about how light
interacts with the surface, not

00:01:46.535 --> 00:01:48.035
the surface itself.

00:01:48.035 --> 00:01:51.270
So as we know, when you look
at a, you know, an object in

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.830
three d, there's no such
thing as a curved surface.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:55.430
It's all flat.

00:01:55.430 --> 00:01:56.950
Right? They're always flat.

00:01:56.970 --> 00:01:58.470
They're always triangles.

00:01:58.470 --> 00:01:59.970
A triangle is always flat.

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:02.035
We don't have a
curve happening here.

00:02:02.035 --> 00:02:05.315
We have something that looks
like a curve happening here.

00:02:05.315 --> 00:02:07.715
It looks like it's round.

00:02:07.715 --> 00:02:10.915
And it looks like it's
round because of normal.

00:02:10.915 --> 00:02:12.933
So if I just select both of

00:02:12.933 --> 00:02:14.950
these and you'll see that we've

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:18.470
got normals sticking out of
the surfaces of these two.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:21.030
And I'm just gonna go
into normal mode here.

00:02:21.030 --> 00:02:23.590
And I can make this box

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.150
suddenly look like it is the,

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:27.670
the sphere very quickly.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:30.965
If I grab all the normals of
the box, these little arrows,

00:02:30.965 --> 00:02:33.525
those little normalized
normals, right, that we

00:02:33.525 --> 00:02:36.965
normally talk about, and I go
and unify all those, you'll

00:02:36.965 --> 00:02:40.005
suddenly notice that the
lighting of the box becomes

00:02:40.005 --> 00:02:41.505
very soft.

00:02:41.505 --> 00:02:43.680
Those edges are gone, right?

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:46.200
And likewise, if I take all

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.720
the normals of the,
sphere and I

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:50.400
break all those, you'll notice

00:02:50.400 --> 00:02:53.200
now it looks like
it's all faceted.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:55.785
So now we're seeing all
the flat faces of it.

00:02:55.785 --> 00:02:58.585
So I've kind of
inverted the two models.

00:02:58.585 --> 00:03:00.665
The box looks more like the
sphere did, and the sphere

00:03:00.665 --> 00:03:02.165
looks more like the box did as

00:03:02.165 --> 00:03:03.665
far as the lighting's concerned.

00:03:03.665 --> 00:03:06.425
So what we're seeing then in
you know, if we look at the

00:03:06.425 --> 00:03:09.170
box, it's a little easier,
and I can grab a corner here.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:12.850
This is there are three
triangles that come together at

00:03:12.850 --> 00:03:14.610
that point, at that corner, and

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.090
each of those
vertices has a normal.

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:19.010
And all of those normals are

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.615
pointing exactly
the same direction.

00:03:21.615 --> 00:03:24.735
So as the light comes in and
hits really close to that

00:03:24.735 --> 00:03:26.775
corner or right on the corner,

00:03:26.775 --> 00:03:28.815
it's bouncing exactly the same

00:03:28.815 --> 00:03:32.415
direction for each of the
triangles right at the corner.

00:03:32.415 --> 00:03:35.455
And then as it starts to curve,
you know, that hits further

00:03:35.455 --> 00:03:38.190
down the box, it's taking
an average of the normal.

00:03:38.190 --> 00:03:41.870
So if the normals are pointing
like that on the bottom corner

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:43.950
and the upper corner, the light

00:03:43.950 --> 00:03:45.450
in the middle is
taking a normal.

00:03:45.450 --> 00:03:47.630
So it's taking the average
of those normals or weighted

00:03:47.630 --> 00:03:50.110
average, a barycentric
coordinate, I guess it would

00:03:50.110 --> 00:03:53.805
be, across the surface of the
triangle to figure out where

00:03:53.805 --> 00:03:56.525
the normal is over that surface.

00:03:56.525 --> 00:03:58.565
And it's making the surface

00:03:58.565 --> 00:04:00.605
look like it's curved because

00:04:00.605 --> 00:04:01.933
the light's bouncing at

00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:03.260
different angles as it goes

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.420
over this flat triangle.

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.820
-Right. So instead of the
normals all pointing the same

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.300
way, they're being
averaged out.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:13.420
-Our flat table experiment, we
could actually make this look

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.900
like, at the right angles, it

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:17.025
was actually looking at a ball.

00:04:17.025 --> 00:04:18.525
You know, it would look curved.

00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:22.285
But if you look at it flat
on, of course, we notice it's

00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:23.785
still flat.

00:04:23.785 --> 00:04:25.285
Right? It's still flat.

00:04:25.285 --> 00:04:27.965
If I break these, you'll
suddenly notice I start getting

00:04:27.965 --> 00:04:31.005
those hard corners back, and
now you can see I've got three

00:04:31.005 --> 00:04:32.505
normals there.

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:34.320
So I've broken the
three vertex normals.

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:35.980
They're now pointing

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.800
perpendicular to
their own triangles.

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:42.560
So when the light hits right on
that very, very corner, little

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:44.800
to one triangle, a little to
the other, a little to the

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:46.880
other, it's bouncing
in completely different

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:49.815
directions, making us look
like we have a corner.

00:04:49.815 --> 00:04:53.175
But as you see, as it blends
off to this corner, you'll see

00:04:53.175 --> 00:04:54.935
that it comes back
together again.

00:04:54.935 --> 00:04:57.015
So if I grab that one and go

00:04:57.015 --> 00:04:59.095
break, now that corner of the

00:04:59.095 --> 00:05:02.770
box is looking like it's okay.

00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:06.770
So we can actually mess with
normals in some fun ways.

00:05:06.770 --> 00:05:08.450
I've got a flat plane here.

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:11.730
So as you can imagine, the flat
plane with the lighting on it

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.450
is gonna look just
kinda like a flat plane.

00:05:14.450 --> 00:05:15.950
Alright. It's dead flat.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:17.450
We can see it's dead flat.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:21.275
Now it has it's kinda hard to
see, but you can see that it's

00:05:21.275 --> 00:05:22.775
got there you go.

00:05:22.775 --> 00:05:24.275
When I select it, it's got a

00:05:24.275 --> 00:05:26.075
certain amount of
segments to it.

00:05:26.075 --> 00:05:29.195
So what I did down here in this
normal, I actually took the

00:05:29.195 --> 00:05:30.715
normals and I can bend them.

00:05:30.715 --> 00:05:33.158
So I can pick those, normals

00:05:33.158 --> 00:05:35.600
and I can manipulate the

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.100
normals believe it or not.

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.720
So I can actually
change their direction.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.560
So with that on, you'll now

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:44.400
notice the light starts casting

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:45.900
off the edge.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:48.575
So because these normals are
pointing away from the light

00:05:48.575 --> 00:05:52.175
more, okay, it thinks that edge

00:05:52.175 --> 00:05:54.735
is curving away from that light.

00:05:54.735 --> 00:05:56.942
So if I pick my light now and

00:05:56.942 --> 00:05:59.150
move it around the surface,

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:01.310
it's looks like it's a curved

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:03.950
surface, but it's
not a curved surface.

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:06.990
It's a flat surface, and we're
just telling the light to

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:10.190
bounce as though it
was a curved surface.

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:11.690
We're cheating it.

00:06:11.690 --> 00:06:13.190
I refer to it as
natural normals.

00:06:13.190 --> 00:06:15.105
I think you called it the
geometry normals is how you

00:06:15.105 --> 00:06:17.985
refer to the these normals
we're talking about here.

00:06:17.985 --> 00:06:21.505
So at each at each corner of
every triangle, there's a set

00:06:21.505 --> 00:06:23.760
there's a normal for each one.

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.640
But we need to go further when
we're doing modeling and stuff

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:28.480
and we're doing
the things we're doing.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.720
So if we're building a
character, for instance, it's

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:34.560
so complicated, we actually
use software like ZBrush that

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:38.000
allows us to sculpt literally
tens of millions of polygons.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:41.495
It's when you go wireframe,
you're looking at a shaded

00:06:41.495 --> 00:06:42.995
viewport still.

00:06:42.995 --> 00:06:44.495
It's just dense with polygons.

00:06:44.535 --> 00:06:47.415
So it's completely
unusable in production.

00:06:47.415 --> 00:06:49.548
We can't actually use that, but

00:06:49.548 --> 00:06:51.680
what we can do is we can steal

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:53.180
normals off it.

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:56.000
So what I've got here is a
representation of that super

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:57.500
high res mesh.

00:06:57.500 --> 00:06:59.000
It isn't super high res, but

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:01.440
you can see that it
is actually deformed.

00:07:01.520 --> 00:07:04.960
It has, you know, a noise
modifier on it that's just

00:07:04.960 --> 00:07:06.460
making it noisy.

00:07:06.460 --> 00:07:10.055
Okay. And I've got this
flat plane down here.

00:07:10.055 --> 00:07:12.215
Now I'm just gonna
turn this one off.

00:07:12.215 --> 00:07:16.215
I'm just gonna the normals
are set back to normal.

00:07:16.215 --> 00:07:18.650
See what it did there?

00:07:18.650 --> 00:07:21.210
Okay. I'm using a
projection modifier.

00:07:21.210 --> 00:07:24.330
We don't need to get into sort
of heavily how that works, but

00:07:24.330 --> 00:07:28.170
essentially what we do from
this little blue cage, which

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.690
represents the low res, we're
gonna fire little ray guns.

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:35.015
For every pixel of the texture
map, we're gonna fire a little

00:07:35.015 --> 00:07:38.215
ray gun straight down
from the blue cage.

00:07:38.215 --> 00:07:40.135
We're gonna hit
the high res one.

00:07:40.135 --> 00:07:42.935
We're gonna figure out the
angle of the high res one.

00:07:42.935 --> 00:07:44.775
We're gonna hit the low res one.

00:07:44.775 --> 00:07:47.830
We're gonna get its normal at
that point where that pixel

00:07:47.830 --> 00:07:50.950
would sit, and we're gonna
compare the angle of the high

00:07:50.950 --> 00:07:53.430
res one with the, you know,

00:07:53.430 --> 00:07:55.910
geometry normal, as you call

00:07:55.910 --> 00:07:59.110
it, and we're gonna create a
tangent space normal texture

00:07:59.110 --> 00:08:02.365
map, meaning two
tangent of that.

00:08:02.365 --> 00:08:04.445
So we're gonna draw a
little coloured pixel.

00:08:04.445 --> 00:08:06.725
Red is one way, green is

00:08:06.725 --> 00:08:09.005
the other, blue, no change.

00:08:09.005 --> 00:08:11.645
Okay? So we're gonna get this
nice little pretty picture

00:08:11.645 --> 00:08:13.145
kinda drawn.

00:08:13.145 --> 00:08:14.645
And what happens now is when
the light comes in like you

00:08:14.645 --> 00:08:18.120
said, it's gonna get the actual
natural normal of it, and then

00:08:18.120 --> 00:08:21.080
it's gonna go look colour pixel
normal is underneath there.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:25.080
I need to bend the I need to
bend that normal in one of

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.000
those little directions
and bend it around.

00:08:27.000 --> 00:08:30.935
Once that's done, and we have
this, I'm just gonna go turn

00:08:30.935 --> 00:08:34.775
that cage back off again,
and I'm gonna go in and plug in

00:08:34.775 --> 00:08:36.275
the normal map.

00:08:37.815 --> 00:08:39.975
And what you'll see now is is

00:08:39.975 --> 00:08:43.095
that as I move this
around.

00:08:43.095 --> 00:08:45.728
So we stole the normals out

00:08:45.728 --> 00:08:48.360
of the high res and basically

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:50.440
baked it down into a texture

00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:52.520
map of pixel colours, and we're

00:08:52.520 --> 00:08:55.352
just using the, you know, the

00:08:55.352 --> 00:08:58.185
RGB as x y z vector changes.

00:08:58.185 --> 00:09:01.225
Right? So we can go in and
change how this. This is

00:09:01.225 --> 00:09:03.385
referred to as a
tangent space normal.

00:09:03.385 --> 00:09:06.265
Now if you wonder what that
really looks like sort of on

00:09:06.265 --> 00:09:08.505
the mesh, I'll just plug
it in as the base colour.

00:09:08.505 --> 00:09:12.000
So that's the pretty colours
that have been generated.

00:09:12.000 --> 00:09:15.600
And essentially,
blues, no change.

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:17.680
Greens, one way.

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.680
Reds, the other way, and it

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:22.480
just sort of deviates
how it looks.

00:09:22.480 --> 00:09:25.760
So we can take models that
are potentially fairly low

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:27.957
resolution and make them look

00:09:27.957 --> 00:09:30.155
like they have all of this

00:09:30.155 --> 00:09:33.515
extra fine detail in them,
everywhere down to pores of

00:09:33.515 --> 00:09:35.755
skin depending on how many
pixels you've got in your

00:09:35.755 --> 00:09:37.255
texture maps.

00:09:37.255 --> 00:09:38.755
Now, of course, when you see

00:09:38.755 --> 00:09:40.550
them edge on,
they're still flat.

00:09:40.550 --> 00:09:42.050
There's nothing there.

00:09:42.050 --> 00:09:43.990
There's no actual
change in surface.

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:45.490
-Optical illusion?

00:09:45.490 --> 00:09:48.950
-Yeah. So we model things so
they have the sort of the big

00:09:48.950 --> 00:09:51.558
or the large medium sized form

00:09:51.558 --> 00:09:54.165
shapes to them, and then we get

00:09:54.165 --> 00:09:58.005
all of the medium small to
small detail out of normal maps

00:09:58.005 --> 00:09:59.505
and cheat it all.

00:09:59.505 --> 00:10:02.885
-Wow. And does that really help
with the, kind of how much

00:10:02.885 --> 00:10:05.525
computer time you need to,
like, render these objects?

00:10:05.525 --> 00:10:07.750
-Well, yeah, in
all sorts of ways.

00:10:07.750 --> 00:10:10.470
I mean, you wanna load a
model that's, you know,

00:10:10.470 --> 00:10:13.750
20,000,000,000 polygons or
whatever we do when we sculpt.

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:15.250
No. You don't.

00:10:15.250 --> 00:10:17.110
Right? It would just
crunch everything to a halt.

00:10:17.110 --> 00:10:19.363
In this case, for games, we can

00:10:19.363 --> 00:10:21.615
load a very low res, you know,

00:10:21.615 --> 00:10:24.975
model and put normal maps on
it and the right textures and

00:10:24.975 --> 00:10:27.855
everything and even cheat
a little bit by painting some

00:10:27.855 --> 00:10:30.095
shadows into things that
we want and everything.

00:10:30.095 --> 00:10:32.495
And we can cheat it out and
make it look like it is really

00:10:32.495 --> 00:10:33.995
high detailed.

00:10:33.995 --> 00:10:35.820
Next time you're playing a
video game, walk around an

00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:37.900
object that looks high detailed

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:39.980
and look at it on angles, then

00:10:39.980 --> 00:10:41.480
make it go and you'll suddenly

00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:42.980
realize you're
looking at flat faces.

00:10:42.980 --> 00:10:45.660
-Oh, I'm not gonna be able to
look at video games the same

00:10:45.660 --> 00:10:47.160
way now.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:48.660
-No. We ruin them for everybody.

00:10:50.160 --> 00:10:51.660
-Yeah.

00:10:51.660 --> 00:10:53.265
Yeah. So maybe I could just
summarize what we've been

00:10:53.265 --> 00:10:54.765
talking about today.

00:10:54.785 --> 00:10:58.705
Normals. It's a bit of a weird
word, but it just means 90

00:10:58.705 --> 00:11:00.465
degrees to the surface.

00:11:00.465 --> 00:11:02.617
You got an x, a y, and a z,

00:11:02.617 --> 00:11:04.770
and you set that last component

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:06.450
w to zero.

00:11:06.450 --> 00:11:08.530
